    {
      "type": "text",
      "name": "question",
      "text": "What did I actually\nsee—and what did I\nadd?",
      "x": 60,
      "y": 820,
      "size": 33,
      "font": "serif",
      "color": "#9fecdf",
      "line_height": 1.25
    },
    {
      "type": "rich-text",
      "name": "essay-01",
      "markdown": "The contemporary “heyoka empath” is frequently imagined as an emotional mirror: someone who reflects a feeling, contradiction, or defense back to another person. This is the organizing metaphor of the modern archetype. It gives a name to the experience of feeling seen, unsettled, or suddenly aware of a pattern.\n\nThe metaphor is compelling because recognition can arrive indirectly. A surprising question or a well-timed joke can reveal the distance between the story we tell about ourselves and the way we behave. The important event is the recognition, rather than the idea that one person has infallible access to another’s inner life.\n\nFor a modern reading, separate three things: the behavior you observed, your interpretation of it, and the other person’s account of their experience. That distinction does not empty the metaphor of meaning. It gives the encounter enough room to be explored instead of settled by a label.",
      "x": 470,
      "y": 280,
      "width": 490,
      "font": "sans",
      "size": 22,
      "line_height": 1.4,
      "color": "#f1eaff"
    },
