    {
      "type": "text",
      "name": "question",
      "text": "Can two truths share\nthe same frame?",
      "x": 60,
      "y": 820,
      "size": 33,
      "font": "serif",
      "color": "#f0c878",
      "line_height": 1.25
    },
    {
      "type": "rich-text",
      "name": "essay-02",
      "markdown": "The modern heyoka archetype is described through tensions: playful and serious, kind and unsettling, perceptive and unpredictable. The contradiction is not a flaw in the image. It is what makes the image attractive to people who feel constrained by a single description of themselves.\n\nParadox can open a useful space. A friend may care about you and challenge you. A joke may feel affectionate and still miss its mark. A person can be emotionally sensitive while misreading a situation. Holding both possibilities can produce a more precise conversation than assigning one fixed identity.\n\nAs a reflective practice, write two apparently conflicting statements about an encounter. Ask what each explains, and what each leaves out. The modern heyoka lens is useful when it loosens an assumption. It becomes less useful when contradiction is treated as proof that every action must contain special wisdom.",
      "x": 470,
      "y": 280,
      "width": 490,
      "font": "sans",
      "size": 22,
      "line_height": 1.4,
      "color": "#fff0d0"
    },
