    {
      "type": "text",
      "name": "question",
      "text": "Did the laugh reveal a\npattern—or hide a\ncriticism?",
      "x": 60,
      "y": 820,
      "size": 33,
      "font": "serif",
      "color": "#ffb9d6",
      "line_height": 1.25
    },
    {
      "type": "rich-text",
      "name": "essay-03",
      "markdown": "Modern descriptions of heyoka often give humor a disruptive role. A surprising response changes the emotional rhythm of an exchange. Irony makes a contradiction visible. Exaggeration enlarges a habit until the person who has it can finally recognize it.\n\nThe idea is more interesting than “funny people are wise.” A joke can create distance from a defensive story, but it can also create distance from another person. Its effect depends on timing, relationship, and whether the people involved experience it as an invitation or an attack.\n\nFor reflection, return to the exact joke or gesture. What assumption did it expose? Who was able to laugh? What happened afterward? A modern heyoka interpretation becomes concrete when it can describe those details, instead of using the archetype to turn every provocation into a lesson.",
      "x": 470,
      "y": 280,
      "width": 490,
      "font": "sans",
      "size": 22,
      "line_height": 1.4,
      "color": "#fff0e1"
    },
