    {
      "type": "text",
      "name": "question",
      "text": "Why did this\nparticular moment\nstay with me?",
      "x": 60,
      "y": 820,
      "size": 33,
      "font": "serif",
      "color": "#e2aa91",
      "line_height": 1.25
    },
    {
      "type": "rich-text",
      "name": "essay-04",
      "markdown": "In modern spiritual discussion, “shadow” often names qualities a person struggles to accept or notice in themselves. The heyoka mirror is sometimes imagined as a person who brings those qualities to the surface. The encounter may feel irritating, magnetic, or difficult to forget.\n\nA useful reflection starts with the reaction itself. What did I feel? What value, fear, or expectation seemed threatened? Does this resemble a pattern I know? These questions explore the experience without deciding in advance that the other person possesses a special ability to expose it.\n\nProjection is a possibility, not the automatic explanation for every disagreement. Sometimes a reaction reflects a real breach of trust or a boundary that needs attention. The modern shadow-mirror lens should help a person see more clearly, including when the clearest conclusion is that an interaction was simply unwelcome.",
      "x": 470,
      "y": 280,
      "width": 490,
      "font": "sans",
      "size": 22,
      "line_height": 1.4,
      "color": "#eee3ed"
