    {
      "type": "text",
      "name": "question",
      "text": "What became\npossible when the\nusual response\nstopped?",
      "x": 60,
      "y": 820,
      "size": 33,
      "font": "serif",
      "color": "#8fe0d2",
      "line_height": 1.25
    },
    {
      "type": "rich-text",
      "name": "essay-05",
      "markdown": "Modern heyoka language often describes someone who does not follow the expected social script. A familiar response is replaced by an unexpected question, an absurd comparison, or a moment of silence. The break can make a routine reaction suddenly visible.\n\nThis image connects with a general creative technique: pattern interruption. Writers reverse an expected ending. Comedians change the premise. Designers alter scale or visual hierarchy. In each case, surprise draws attention to a structure the audience had stopped noticing.\n\nFor reflection, identify the old script before praising the interruption. What normally happens? What changed? Did the change produce a clearer understanding or only confusion? The modern heyoka lens becomes more specific when it explains what the interruption made possible.",
      "x": 470,
      "y": 280,
      "width": 490,
      "font": "sans",
      "size": 22,
      "line_height": 1.4,
      "color": "#e8f3ff"
    },
