    {
      "type": "text",
      "name": "question",
      "text": "Can I stay curious\nwithout deciding who\nsomeone is?",
      "x": 60,
      "y": 820,
      "size": 33,
      "font": "serif",
      "color": "#efd09a",
      "line_height": 1.25
    },
    {
      "type": "rich-text",
      "name": "essay-06",
      "markdown": "The grounded version of the modern heyoka metaphor begins with relationship. Reflection is not something one person does to another from a position of unquestionable insight. It is an exchange in which both people can explain, disagree, correct, and choose their boundaries.\n\nA response may help someone notice a pattern. That does not make every response helpful, and it does not remove responsibility for its impact. A joke can be repaired. An assumption can be withdrawn. An interpretation can change after the other person speaks.\n\nUse the archetype as a lens that adds curiosity rather than a verdict that ends the conversation. “I noticed something—does it fit your experience?” offers a more grounded mirror than “I know the lesson you need.” Care gives the metaphor somewhere to stand.",
      "x": 470,
      "y": 280,
      "width": 490,
      "font": "sans",
      "size": 22,
      "line_height": 1.4,
      "color": "#f5eddb"
    },
    {
      "type": "shape",
      "shape": "rectangle",
      "name": "line",
      "x": 60,
      "y": 1240,
      "width": 900,
      "height": 2,
      "fill": "#efd09a"
    },
    {
      "type": "layer-intent",
      "target": "line",
