    {
      "type": "text",
      "name": "body",
      "text": "The traditional Lakota heyoka role and the modern “heyoka empath”\narchetype are distinct contexts. This publication explores the latter:\na contemporary spiritual interpretation organized around emotional\nmirroring, paradox, humor, and reflection.\n\nThese essays are interpretive writing, not clinical findings. They do\nnot verify special powers or establish spiritual recognition. They\nexplore what the modern metaphors can mean in ordinary\nexperiences and relationships.\n\nFor cultural grounding, the WoLakota Project’s Lakota Pronunciation\nGlossary gives heyoka as “a sacred clown.” Black Elk Speaks (1932),\nchapter 16, “Heyoka Ceremony,” is a historical account to read in its\nown context. The WoLakota Project also offers Elder interviews and\nthe Oceti Sakowin Essential Understandings.",
      "x": 60,
